	<!--
		Declared here rather than CreateFrame'd in Party.mixin.lua, which is the
		one thing that made this holder different from the five above it.

		PartyFrame is reparented onto this frame. A frame created by Lua gets an
		insecure global - /df log party said so in as many words,
		"DragonflightUIPartyMoveFrame GLOBAL insecure, tainted by
		DragonflightUI" - and hanging the party container off it left Blizzard
		tainting itself while updating the members, so pooled member .unit came
		out insecure and Show/SetAttribute were refused. The XML loader creates
		these, so the global is secure, which is why the player, pet, target and
		focus holders have never had this problem.

		Not hidden - and neither is any holder above it, despite what the focus
		target module used to claim. They are only ever anchors, and what is
		anchored to them has to stay visible.
	-->
	<Frame name="DragonflightUIPartyMoveFrame" inherits="SecureFrameTemplate,SecureHandlerEnterLeaveTemplate">
	</Frame>
	<!--
		Same reasoning as the party holder above, and for the same reason it has to
		be XML: CompactRaidFrameContainer is reparented onto this one, and it is
		protected. A Lua-created global would be insecure, Blizzard would taint
		itself while updating the raid members, and the member frames would start
		refusing Show and SetAttribute - exactly what happened to the pooled party
		members.

		The raid edit mode selection used to hang off Blizzard's
		CompactRaidFrameManagerContainerResizeFrame instead. That frame sits under
		CompactRaidFrameManager, which the client keeps hidden now that raid frames
		are an Edit Mode system, and it carries no anchor points at all - so the
		selection was invisible and unplaceable. /df log frame reported it as
		"shown=true visible=false points=0".

		Not hidden: this is only ever an anchor, and what hangs off it must stay
		visible.
	-->
	<Frame name="DragonflightUIRaidMoveFrame" inherits="SecureFrameTemplate,SecureHandlerEnterLeaveTemplate">
	</Frame>
</Ui>
